statement:
Let $p, q, r$ be positive real numbers, not all equal, such that some two of the equations
\[
px^2 + 2qx + r = 0, \qquad qx^2 + 2rx + p = 0, \qquad rx^2 + 2px + q = 0
\]
have a common root, say $\alpha$. Prove that
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $\alpha$ is real and negative;
\item[(b)] the remaining third quadratic equation has non-real roots.
\end{enumerate}

Let $x_1$ be a given positive integer. A sequence $\{x_n\}_{n \geq 1}$ of positive integers is such that $x_n$, for $n \geq 2$, is obtained from $x_{n-1}$ by adding some nonzero digit of $x_{n-1}$. Prove that
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] the sequence contains an even term;
\item[(b)] the sequence contains infinitely many even terms.
\end{enumerate}
